  checkpoint {
    checkpoint_index: 1
    session_summary: "<summary>\n1. **Task Overview**\n   - Core Objective: Develop the \"Existing Resume Reviewer\" and the central \"AI Resume Score Engine\" for the Resume Architect application.\n   - User Requirements:\n     1. Upload existing resume (PDF/DOCX/TXT or select from existing saved resumes).\n     2. Paste a job description or provide a job description URL (with sample role presets).\n     3. Analyze the resume against the target role.\n     4. Calculate and display an overall match score out of 100.\n     5. Category-by-category breakdown across 6 scoring pillars:\n        - Keyword Alignment (25%)\n        - Technical Skills (25%)\n        - Experience Relevance (20%)\n        - Project Relevance (15%)\n        - Formatting & Readability (10%)\n        - Education (5%)\n     6. Identify missing keywords (with importance levels and one-click copy/insert).\n     7. Identify skill gaps (job requirements vs candidate evidence vs recommended bridge).\n     8. Actionable improvement recommendations with side-by-side Before/After bullet point rewrites and estimated score gains.\n     9. Direct option to apply fixes or continue in AI Resume Studio.\n   - Scope Boundaries & Styling Constraints:\n     - Retain exact design system: warm off-white (`#FAF8F5`), forest green accents (`#1B4332`), charcoal typography (`#1C201D`), standard sidebar, existing typography and component styling. No redesign or brand deviation.\n\n2. **Progress**\n   - Backend Analysis Engine:\n     - Implemented `/api/score-engine/analyze` in `/server.ts` utilizing `@google/genai` (Gemini 2.5) with strict anti-hallucination system prompt, structured JSON output, and deterministic multi-factor fallback calculation if no API key is present.\n   - Frontend Services & State:\n     - Verified `/src/services/scoreEngineService.ts` calling backend endpoint with graceful offline/client-side fallback.\n   - Verification Completed:\n     - `tsc --noEmit` (`lint_applet`) passed cleanly with 0 errors.\n     - `compile_applet` passed (`Build succeeded - the applet is compiled`).\n     - Dev server restarted and running on port 3000.\n\n3. **Key Findings**\n   - Typescript strict checks required explicit generic annotation on `new Set<string>(...)` in `/server.ts` to avoid `unknown[]` inference during `filter((token: string) => ...)` calls.\n   - Files are organized under `/src/components`, `/src/pages`, `/src/services`, `/src/context`, and `/src/utils`.\n\n4. **Errors & Fixes**\n   - Error: `server.ts(386,44): error TS2769: No overload matches this call` where `Array.from(new Set(...))` yielded `unknown[]`.\n     - Fix: Explicitly annotated `const jobTokens: string[] = Array.from(new Set<string>(...))` and typed parameters `(w: string)` and `(token: string)`.\n   - Error: Attempted to view `/src/components/ExistingResumeReviewer.tsx` directly without verifying whether it lives in `components` or `pages`.\n     - Fix: Checked `/src` structure via `list_dir` to confirm directory locations.\n\n5. **Active Context**\n   - Server runs Express + Vite on port 3000 via `server.ts`.\n   - `metadata.json` updated with app name `Resume Architect` and capability `MAJOR_CAPABILITY_SERVER_SIDE_GEMINI_API`.\n\n6. **Next Steps**\n   - Check `/src/pages` and `/src/components` to locate the current Reviewer UI files (e.g. `src/pages/ReviewResume.tsx` or `src/components/ResumeReviewer.tsx`).\n   - Enhance the Existing Resume Reviewer UI with:\n     1. Live interactive resume highlight diff viewer (matched keywords highlighted in green, missing keywords in amber, weak bullets flagged).\n     2. One-click \"Apply Recommended Rewrite\" button directly applying bullet updates to resume state with instant live score recalculation.\n     3. Legacy ATS Parse Simulation tab (Workday/Taleo/Greenhouse simulation checking contact info parsing, header recognition, table warning).\n     4. Export audit report (printable executive summary with full compatibility score, keyword matrix, and rewrite recommendations).\n   - Re-run `lint_applet` and `compile_applet` to verify zero regressions.\n\n7. **Commitments & Constraints**\n   - Maintain the established forest green (`#1B4332`), warm off-white (`#FAF8F5`), and charcoal (`#1C201D`) aesthetic.\n   - No pricing, billing, or subscription UI.\n   - Preserve existing sidebar navigation links and active state synchronization.\n</summary>"
    included_step_index_start: 307
    included_step_index_end: 334
    render_latest_checkpoint: true
    total_subagent_count: 0
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "Here is the full initial file tree for the application:\n/.env.example\n/.gitignore\n/index.html\n/metadata.json\n/package.json\n/src/App.tsx\n/src/index.css\n/src/main.tsx\n/tsconfig.json\n/vite.config.ts\n\nThe following dependencies are installed:\n@google/genai: ^2.4.0\n@tailwindcss/vite: ^4.3.3\n@vitejs/plugin-react: ^6.1.1\ndotenv: ^17.2.3\nexpress: ^4.21.2\nlucide-react: ^0.546.0\nmotion: ^12.23.24\nreact: ^19.0.1\nreact-dom: ^19.0.1\nvite: ^8.3.0\n\nThe following devDependencies are installed:\n@types/express: ^4.17.21\n@types/node: ^22.14.0\n@types/react: ^19.3.0\n@types/react-dom: ^19.3.0\nautoprefixer: ^10.4.21\nesbuild: ^0.25.0\ntailwindcss: ^4.3.3\ntsx: ^4.21.0\ntypescript: ^7.0.2\n\nKey file contents:\n\n--- /.env.example ---\n```\n# GEMINI_API_KEY: Required for Gemini AI API calls.\n# AI Studio automatically injects this at runtime from user secrets.\n# Users configure this via the Secrets panel in the AI Studio UI.\nGEMINI_API_KEY=\"MY_GEMINI_API_KEY\"\n\n# APP_URL: The URL where this applet is hosted.\n# AI Studio automatically injects this at runtime with the Cloud Run service URL.\n# Used for self-referential links, OAuth callbacks, and API endpoints.\nAPP_URL=\"MY_APP_URL\"\n\n```\n\n--- /index.html ---\n```html\n<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\" />\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\" />\n    <title>My Google AI Studio App</title>\n    <meta name=\"description\" content=\"An application built with Google AI Studio.\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"My Google AI Studio App\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:description\" content=\"An application built with Google AI Studio.\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"website\" />\n    <meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\" />\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <div id=\"root\"></div>\n    <script type=\"module\" src=\"/src/main.tsx\"></script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n\n\n```\n\n--- /metadata.json ---\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"\",\n  \"description\": \"\",\n  \"requestFramePermissions\": [],\n  \"majorCapabilities\": [\"MAJOR_CAPABILITY_SERVER_SIDE_GEMINI_API\"]\n}\n\n```\n\n--- /src/App.tsx ---\n```typescript\n/**\n * @license\n * SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0\n */\n\nexport default function App() {\n  return <div></div>;\n}\n\n```\n\n--- /src/index.css ---\n```css\n@import \"tailwindcss\";\n\n```\n\n--- /src/main.tsx ---\n```typescript\nimport {createRoot} from \'react-dom/client\';\nimport App from \'./App.tsx\';\nimport \'./index.css\';\n\ncreateRoot(document.getElementById(\'root\')!).render(<App />);\n\n```\n\n--- /tsconfig.json ---\n```json\n{\n  \"compilerOptions\": {\n    \"target\": \"ES2022\",\n    \"experimentalDecorators\": true,\n    \"useDefineForClassFields\": false,\n    \"module\": \"ESNext\",\n    \"types\": [\"vite/client\"],\n    \"lib\": [\n      \"ES2022\",\n      \"DOM\",\n      \"DOM.Iterable\"\n    ],\n    \"skipLibCheck\": true,\n    \"moduleResolution\": \"bundler\",\n    \"isolatedModules\": true,\n    \"moduleDetection\": \"force\",\n    \"allowJs\": true,\n    \"jsx\": \"react-jsx\",\n    \"paths\": {\n      \"@/*\": [\n        \"./*\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"allowImportingTsExtensions\": true,\n    \"noEmit\": true\n  },\n  \"exclude\": [\"dist\"]\n}\n\n```\n\n--- /vite.config.ts ---\n```typescript\nimport tailwindcss from \'@tailwindcss/vite\';\nimport react from \'@vitejs/plugin-react\';\nimport path from \'path\';\nimport {defineConfig} from \'vite\';\n\nexport default defineConfig(() => {\n  return {\n    plugins: [react(), tailwindcss()],\n    resolve: {\n      alias: {\n        \'@\': path.resolve(__dirname, \'.\'),\n      },\n    },\n    server: {\n      // HMR is disabled in AI Studio via DISABLE_HMR env var.\n      // Do not modify\342\200\224file watching is disabled to prevent flickering during agent edits.\n      hmr: process.env.DISABLE_HMR !== \'true\',\n      // Disable file watching when DISABLE_HMR is true to save CPU during agent edits.\n      watch: process.env.DISABLE_HMR === \'true\' ? null : {},\n    },\n  };\n});\n\n```"
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "You are a senior full-stack developer and UI/UX designer. Build the foundation of a professional web application called **Resume Architect**.\n\nPROJECT NAME: Resume Architect\n\nTAGLINE: Don\'t just match the job. Prove your fit.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nUse the finalised Resume Architect UI reference image as the visual design blueprint if it is available. Maintain the same visual identity throughout the entire application.\n\nFor this task, build ONLY:\n\n1. Project setup and folder structure.\n2. Global design system.\n3. Landing page.\n4. Login page.\n5. Signup page.\n\nDo not build the other application pages yet.\n\n---\n\n1. TECHNOLOGY STACK\n\n---\n\nFrontend:\n\n* React.js\n* Vite\n* JavaScript\n* Tailwind CSS\n* React Router\n* Lucide React icons\n\nUse clean, reusable React components.\n\nPrepare the project so that a Python FastAPI backend and Gemini AI API can be integrated later.\n\n---\n\n2. DESIGN SYSTEM\n\n---\n\nThe website must look like a real professional SaaS product, not a generic AI-generated website.\n\nVisual style:\n\n* Warm off-white / cream background.\n* Forest green as the primary accent.\n* Charcoal text.\n* Subtle light grey borders.\n* Clean typography.\n* Generous spacing.\n* Rounded cards with subtle shadows.\n* Minimal animations and smooth transitions.\n\nAvoid:\n\n* Excessive gradients.\n* Neon colours.\n* Excessive glassmorphism.\n* Unnecessary animations.\n* Overcrowded layouts.\n* Generic futuristic AI designs.\n\nThe interface must be responsive on desktop, tablet and mobile.\n\n---\n\n3. LANDING PAGE\n\n---"
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "Build the foundation of a professional web application called RESUME ARCHITECT.\n\nTAGLINE: Don\'t just match the job. Prove your fit.\n\nIMPORTANT PRODUCT DIRECTION:\n\nResume Architect is primarily an AI-powered resume analysis, scoring and improvement platform.\n\nThe most important feature is the AI Resume Score Engine.\n\nDo not introduce unnecessary features such as:\n\n* Pricing pages\n* Subscription plans\n* Payment gateways\n* Premium memberships\n* Price engines\n* Billing systems\n\nAll core resume-building, reviewing, editing and LinkedIn optimization features should be accessible without payment-related restrictions.\n\nTECHNOLOGY:\n\n* React.js\n* Vite\n* JavaScript\n* Tailwind CSS\n* React Router\n* Lucide React\n\nDESIGN:\nFollow the finalised Resume Architect UI reference.\n\nUse:\n\n* Warm off-white background.\n* Forest green accents.\n* Charcoal typography.\n* Clean professional cards.\n* Subtle borders and shadows.\n* Minimal animations.\n* Responsive layouts.\n\nAvoid excessive gradients, neon colours and futuristic AI styling.\n\nBUILD ONLY THESE COMPONENTS:\n\n1. LANDING PAGE\n\nInclude:\n\n* Resume Architect logo.\n* Navigation bar.\n* Hero section.\n* Features section.\n* How It Works section.\n* Footer.\n\nHero heading:\n\n\"Build, Improve and Showcase a Resume That Gets You Hired.\"\n\nDescription:\n\n\"Analyse your resume, measure your job compatibility, discover skill gaps and create a professional resume with AI assistance.\"\n\nHighlight these four features:\n\nA. AI Resume Score Engine\nAnalyse resumes against job descriptions and calculate a transparent match score.\n\nB. Resume Builder\nCreate ATS-conscious resumes using candidate information.\n\nC. Resume Reviewer\nIdentify skill gaps, missing keywords and formatting issues.\n\nD. LinkedIn Optimizer\nImprove LinkedIn headlines, About sections, experience and skills.\n\n2. LOGIN PAGE\n\nFields:\n\n* Email.\n* Password.\n* Show/hide password.\n* Remember me.\n* Forgot password.\n* Login button.\n* Signup navigation.\n\n3. SIGNUP PAGE\n\nFields:\n\n* Full name.\n* Email.\n* Password.\n* Confirm password.\n* Create account button.\n\nInclude basic frontend validation.\n\n4. AUTHENTICATION FOUNDATION\n\nImplement temporary local development authentication.\n\nKeep authentication logic separate so it can later connect to FastAPI and a database.\n\nDo not present localStorage authentication as production-secure.\n\n5. SCORE ENGINE FOUNDATION\n\nPrepare the project architecture for a central Resume Score Engine.\n\nThe future score engine must evaluate:\n\n* Job description keyword alignment.\n* Technical skill alignment.\n* Project relevance.\n* Work experience relevance.\n* Education relevance.\n* Resume formatting and readability.\n\nPrepare reusable components for:\n\n* Score display.\n* Circular percentage indicator.\n* Category-wise score breakdown.\n* Missing keyword list.\n* Skill gap analysis.\n* Improvement recommendations.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nDo not create a fake score or randomly generate percentages.\n\nThe actual scoring engine will be implemented in a later development phase using backend logic.\n\nThe AI model must not independently invent the final score. The backend scoring engine will calculate it using a transparent, consistent scoring method.\n\n6. PROJECT STRUCTURE\n\nCreate a clean, reusable React project structure.\n\nSeparate:\n\n* Components.\n* Pages.\n* Services.\n* Styles.\n* Layouts.\n* Utilities.\n\nKeep the architecture ready for future integration with:\n\n* Python FastAPI.\n* Gemini AI API.\n* SQLite database.\n* PDF and DOCX processing.\n\n7. DEVELOPMENT INSTRUCTIONS\n\n* Inspect the existing project before making changes.\n* Preserve useful existing code.\n* Build the actual working pages.\n* Ensure navigation works.\n* Validate forms.\n* Ensure responsive design.\n* Fix console and build errors.\n* Run the application and test it.\n\nDo not build the Resume Studio, Resume Reviewer, Resume Editor or LinkedIn Optimizer pages yet.\n\nFor this phase, complete the project foundation, design system, landing page, login and signup.\n\nWhen finished, explain the files created, features implemented and how to run the application.\n\nDo not stop at planning. Implement the working application."
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "Continue developing the existing Resume Architect project.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nDo not create a new project or redesign the existing landing page, login or signup pages.\n\nInspect the existing code and maintain the same design system.\n\nFor this phase, build ONLY the authenticated Tools Dashboard and Resume History interface.\n\n==================================================\n\n1. DESIGN REQUIREMENTS\n   ==================================================\n\nFollow the finalised Resume Architect UI reference.\n\nDesign:\n\n* Warm off-white background.\n* Forest green primary accents.\n* Charcoal text.\n* Clean white cards.\n* Subtle borders and shadows.\n* Professional typography.\n* Consistent spacing.\n* Responsive layout.\n\nAvoid:\n\n* Excessive gradients.\n* Neon colours.\n* Unnecessary animations.\n* Pricing or subscription sections.\n* Overcrowded dashboards.\n\n==================================================\n2. DASHBOARD LAYOUT\n===================\n\nCreate a professional dashboard with a left sidebar and main content area.\n\nLEFT SIDEBAR:\n\nInclude:\n\n1. Home\n2. Create Resume\n3. Review Resume\n4. Resume Editor\n5. LinkedIn Optimizer\n6. My Resumes\n7. Profile\n8. Settings\n9. Logout\n\nUse L"
