  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Observing Recent Updates**\n\nI see that `LinkedInView.tsx` has graduated from placeholder status! Also, User Request 8 is quite directive; the instructions are very clear about continuing the Resume Architect project, and explicitly forbid the creation of any new projects.\n\n\n**Defining Project Scope**\n\nI\'m focused on the scope. I\'m prioritizing the AI-powered LinkedIn Profile Optimizer, following the clear directive of User Request 8. I\'ll maintain the existing design, components, and navigation. The focus is exclusively on the profile optimizer, especially for students, freshers, and professionals. The optimizer will use genuine information, never fabricating achievements. The design centers around a clean dashboard with a \"LinkedIn Profile Optimizer\" title.\n\n\n**Detailing Profile Input**\n\nI\'m now detailing the profile input options. I\'m planning both manual input and import from Resume Architect. For manual input, I\'ll allow entry for personal info, headline, about section, experience, education, projects, skills, and certifications. I need to make the import from Resume Architect user-friendly. The right panel will have an AI analysis and preview. I must also maintain Resume Architect\'s visual identity.\n\n\n**Implementing Data Import**\n\nNow I\'m focusing on data import from the resumes users have already uploaded in Resume Architect. I\'ll automatically populate the profile fields with this data. It\'s essential the user can review and edit this imported data before initiating the optimization.\n\n\n**Analyzing Profile Impact**\n\nI\'m now analyzing LinkedIn profile effectiveness. I\'m focusing on search visibility and recruiter discoverability, separate from Resume Match Score. I\'ll provide a transparent score with an overall score (0-100), category breakdowns, strengths, weaknesses, and actionable recommendations. Profile completeness, representing 10% of the score, hinges on the presence of key sections.\n\nI\'m also working on an AI headline generator, which will create high-impact headlines tailored to the user\'s role. I\'ll offer role & value proposition, keyword-dense, and growth & impact options.\n\n\n**Refining Optimization Details**\n\nI\'m now detailing improvements for the headline. I\'ve got a character counter in place, and a one-click copy and edit system. I\'m also planning to incorporate an \"Accept as Profile Headline\" button. I\'m developing four distinct styles for the \"About\" section: professional, student-friendly, technical, and concise. I\'m thinking of incorporating a diff view to show users the current and optimized sections. I plan to highlight action verbs, keywords, and clarity improvements. Also, for experience bullets, I am trying to implement the STAR method.\n\n\n**Evaluating Evidence Integrity**\n\nI\'m now detailing the system\'s ability to verify data. I\'m building a system to generate a TruthGuard verification badge for each experience bullet, indicating the support level: SUPPORTED (backed by the user\'s raw input), or NEEDS CONFIRMATION, and never fabricating any details. I\'m also ensuring a one-click accept, edit, or reject option is available.\n\nI\'m also working on skills and keyword analysis, comparing the user\'s background to high-demand keywords. I\'ll show evidence status as MATCHED, PARTIALLY MATCHED, or RECOMMENDED TO ADD, and highlight missing but potentially relevant keywords. Finally, I\'m building an interactive LinkedIn-style preview to show the generated profile, complete with the resume architect forest green accent.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
